  "summary": "France is advancing its European Health Data Space implementation with strengthened data sovereignty measures and secondary data use frameworks to support research and innovation. In the U.S., Medicaid's looming funding cuts are forcing health systems to reckon with financial pressures, while new work requirement rules are raising equity concerns for disabled beneficiaries. A federal judge blocked attempts to restrict SNAP benefits from purchasing sugary foods, limiting one policy lever for addressing nutrition and obesity. The Justice Department announced a major health care fraud crackdown charging hundreds, including a Texas doctor in an $89 million scheme involving unnecessary cardiovascular screenings. On the health-tech innovation front, Cadence raised $100 million for its AI-powered chronic disease management platform with backing from Duke Health and Texas Health Resources, while OpenEvidence launched EchoNext, an FDA-cleared AI tool for heart disease detection via EKG. Separately, Eli Lilly granted extraordinary compassionate use access to its experimental obesity drug retatrutide to a single patient, and partnerships like Oura's collaboration with LillyDirect are expanding support ecosystems for GLP-1 users. Rhode Island passed an ambient AI scribe opt-out law as healthcare systems increasingly deploy AI in clinical workflows, amid broader industry discussions about balancing innovation with transparency and trust.",
